\textbf{2.2} \textbf{Cryptographic Definitions}

The main cryptographic security parameter through the paper is \emph{n},
and all algorithms (including the adversary) are implicitly given the
security parameter \emph{n} in unary.

For a (possibly interactive) algorithm \emph{A}, we define its
\emph{distinguishing advantage} between two distributions \emph{X} and
\emph{Y} to be \emph{\textbar{}}Pr{[}\emph{A}(\emph{X}) = 1{]}
\emph{−}Pr{[}\emph{A}(\emph{Y}) = 1{]}\emph{\textbar{}}. We use the
general notation \textbf{Adv}atk SCH(\emph{A}) to describe the advantage
of an adversary \emph{A} mounting an atk attack on a cryptographic
scheme SCH, where the definition of advantage is specified as part of
the attack. Similarly, we write \textbf{Adv}PROB(\emph{A}) for the
advantage of an
\end{quote}

8

adversary \emph{A} against a computational problem PROB (where again the
meaning of advantage is part of the problem definition).

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Chameleon hash functions.} Chameleon hashing was introduced by
Krawczyk and Rabin {[}KR00{]}. For our purposes, we need a slight
generalization in the spirit of ``preimage sampleable'' (trapdoor)
functions {[}GPV08{]}.

A family of chameleon hash functions is a collection \emph{H} =
\emph{\{hi} : \emph{M × R →Y\}} of functions \emph{hi} mapping a message
\emph{m ∈M} and randomness \emph{r ∈R} to a range \emph{Y}. The
randomness space \emph{R} is endowed with some efficiently sampleable
distribution (which may not be uniform). A function \emph{hi} is
efficiently computable given its description, and the family has the
property that for any \emph{m ∈M}, for \emph{hi ←H} and \emph{r ←R}, the
pair (\emph{hi, hi}(\emph{m, r})) is uniform over (\emph{H, Y}) (up to
negligible statistical distance). The chameleon property is that a
random \emph{hi ←H} may be generated together with a trapdoor \emph{t},
such that for any output \emph{y ∈Y} and message \emph{m ∈M}, it is
possible (using \emph{t}) to efficiently sample \emph{r ∈R} (under the
\emph{R}'s distribution) conditioned on the requirement that
\emph{hi}(\emph{m, r}) = \emph{y}. Finally, the family has the standard
collision-resistance property, i.e., given \emph{hi ←H} it should be
hard for an adversary to find distinct (\emph{m, r})\emph{,} (\emph{m′,
r′}) \emph{∈M × R} such that \emph{hi}(\emph{m, r}) =
\emph{hi}(\emph{m′, r′}).

In Section 4 we realize chameleon hash functions (in the above sense)
using lattices, via bonsai techniques.

\textbf{Signatures.} A signature scheme SIG for a message space \emph{M}
is a tuple of PPT algorithms as follows:

\emph{•} Gen outputs a verification key \emph{vk} and a signing key
\emph{sk}.

\emph{•} Sign(\emph{sk, µ}), given a signing key \emph{sk} and a message
\emph{µ ∈M}, outputs a signature \emph{σ ∈\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}∗}.

\emph{•} Ver(\emph{vk, µ, σ}), given a verification key \emph{vk}, a
message \emph{µ}, and a signature \emph{σ}, either accepts or rejects.
\end{quote}

Ver(\emph{vk, µ, σ}) should accept with overwhelming probability (over
all the randomness in the experiment). The correctness requirement is:
for any \emph{µ ∈M}, generate (\emph{vk, sk}) \emph{←}Gen and \emph{σ
←}Sign(\emph{sk, µ}). Then

\begin{quote}
We recall two standard notions of security for signatures. The first,
existential unforgeability under \emph{static} chosen-message attack, or
eu-scma security, is defined as follows: first, the forger \emph{F}
outputs a list of query messages \emph{µ}1\emph{, . . . , µQ} for some
\emph{Q}. Next, (\emph{vk, sk}) \emph{←}Gen and \emph{σi
←}Sign(\emph{sk, µi}) are generated for each \emph{i ∈}{[}\emph{Q}{]},
then \emph{vk} and \emph{σi} (for each \emph{i ∈}{[}\emph{Q}{]}) are
given to \emph{F}. Finally, \emph{F} outputs an attempted forgery
(\emph{µ∗, σ∗}). The advantage \emph{A}eu-scma taken over all the
randomness of the experiment. (\emph{F}) of \emph{F} is the probability
that Ver(\emph{vk, µ∗, σ∗}) accepts and \emph{µ∗̸}= \emph{µi} for all
\emph{i ∈}{[}\emph{Q}{]},
\end{quote}

Another notion, called existential unforgeability under \emph{adaptive}
chosen-message attack, or eu-acma security, is defined similarly, except
that \emph{F} is first given \emph{vk} and may adaptively choose the
messages \emph{µi}. Using a family of chameleon hash functions (as
defined above), there is a generic construction of eu-acma-secure
signatures from eu-scma-secure signatures; see, e.g., {[}KR00{]}.
Furthermore, the construction results in an \emph{online/offline}
signature scheme; see {[}ST01{]}. The basic idea behind the construction
is that the signer chameleon hashes the message to be signed, then signs
the hashed message using the eu-scma-secure scheme (and includes the
randomness used in the chameleon hash with the final signature).

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Key-Encapsulation Mechanism (KEM).} We present all of our
encryption schemes in the framework of \emph{key encapsulation}, which
simplifies the definitions and leads to more modular constructions. A
KEM for keys of length \emph{ℓ}= \emph{ℓ}(\emph{n}) is a triple of PPT
algorithms as follows:

\emph{•} Gen outputs a public key \emph{pk} and a secret key \emph{sk}.
\end{quote}

9

\begin{quote}
\emph{•} Encaps(\emph{pk}) outputs a key \emph{κ ∈\{}0\emph{,}
1\emph{\}ℓ}and its encapsulation as \emph{σ ∈\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}∗}.

\emph{•} Decaps(\emph{sk, σ}) outputs a key \emph{κ}.
\end{quote}

The correctness requirement is: for (\emph{pk, sk}) \emph{←}Gen and
(\emph{κ, σ}) \emph{←}Encaps(\emph{pk}), Decaps(\emph{sk, σ}) should
output\emph{κ} with all but negl(\emph{n}) probability.

\begin{quote}
In this work we are mainly concerned with indistinguishability under
chosen-plaintext attack, or ind-cpa security. The attack is defined as
follows: generate (\emph{pk, sk}) \emph{←}Gen, (\emph{κ∗, σ∗})
\emph{←}Encaps(\emph{pk}), and \emph{κ′←\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}ℓ}(chosen
uniformly and independently of the other values). The advantage
\textbf{Adv}ind-cpa KEM(\emph{A}) of an adversary \emph{A} is its
distinguishing advantage between (\emph{pk, σ∗, κ∗}) and (\emph{pk, σ∗,
κ′}).

