There are several attack notions for HIBE. One simple notion in
indistinguishability under a chosen-plaintext, \emph{selective-identity}
attack, or sid-ind-cpa security. The attack is defined as follows:
first, the adversary \emph{A} is given 1\emph{d}and names a target
identity \emph{id∗∈ID≤d}. Next, (\emph{mpk, skε})
\emph{←}Setup(1\emph{d}), (\emph{κ, σ∗}) \emph{←}Encaps(\emph{id∗}), and
\emph{κ′←\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}ℓ}are generated. Then \emph{A} is given
(\emph{mpk, κ∗, σ∗}), where \emph{κ∗}is either \emph{κ} or\emph{κ′}.
Finally, \emph{A} may make extraction queries, i.e., it is given oracle
access to Extract(\emph{skε, ·}), subject to the constraint that it may
not query any identity that is a prefix of (or equal to) the target
identity \emph{id∗}. The\emph{κ∗}= \emph{κ′}. HIBE (\emph{A}) is its
distinguishing advantage between the two cases \emph{κ∗}= \emph{κ} and
adversary's advantage \textbf{Adv}sid-ind-cpa

A related notion is security against an \emph{adaptive-identity} attack,
known as aid-ind-cpa or ``full'' security. Here the adversary is first
given \emph{mpk} and has oracle access to Extract(\emph{skε, ·}) both
before and after choosing its target identity \emph{id∗}; as above, the
adversary may never query any identity that is a prefix of the
\emph{id∗}it eventually names. Finally, both of the above notions may be
extended to \emph{chosen-ciphertext} attacks in the natural way; since
our focus is on chosen-plaintext security, we omit precise definitions.

