\textbf{2.3.1} \textbf{Hard Lattices and Problems}
\end{quote}

We will work with an certain family of integer lattices whose importance
in cryptography was first demon-strated by Ajtai {[}Ajt96{]}. Let
\emph{n ≥}1 and modulus \emph{q ≥}2 be integers; the dimension \emph{n}
is the main cryptographic security parameter throughout this work, and
all other parameters are implicitly functions of \emph{n}. An
\emph{m}-dimensional lattice from the family is specified relative to
the additive group Z\emph{n q}by a \emph{parity check} (more accurately,
``arity check'') matrix \textbf{A} \emph{∈}Z\emph{n×m} . The associated
lattice is defined as

\begin{quote}
Λ\emph{⊥}(\textbf{A}) = \textbf{x} \emph{∈}Z\emph{m}: \textbf{Ax} =
\emph{j∈}{[}\emph{m}{]}\emph{xj ·} \textbf{a}\emph{j} = \textbf{0}
\emph{∈}Z\emph{n} \emph{⊆}Z\emph{m.}

One may check that Λ\emph{⊥}(\textbf{A}) contains \emph{q}Z\emph{m}(and
in particular, the identity \textbf{0} \emph{∈}Z\emph{m}) and is closed
under addition, hence it is a full-rank subgroup of (and lattice in)
Z\emph{m}. For any \textbf{y} in the subgroup of Z\emph{n q}generated by
the columns of \textbf{A}, we also define the coset
