We recall the \emph{short integer solution} (SIS) and \emph{learning
with errors} (LWE) problems, which may be seen as average-case problems
related to the family of lattices described above.

\textbf{Definition 2.2} (Short Integer Solution)\textbf{.} An instance
of the SIS\emph{q,β} problem (in the \emph{ℓ}2 norm) is a uniformly
random matrix \textbf{A} \emph{∈}Z\emph{n×m} for any desired \emph{m} =
poly(\emph{n}). The goal is to find a \emph{nonzero} integer vector
\textbf{v} \emph{∈}Z\emph{m} such that \emph{∥}\textbf{v}\emph{∥}2
\emph{≤β} and \textbf{Av} = \textbf{0} \emph{∈}Z\emph{n q}, i.e.,
\textbf{v} \emph{∈}Λ\emph{⊥}(\textbf{A}).
\end{quote}

