In many of our cryptographic applications, a common design pattern is to
extend a basis \textbf{S} of an \emph{m}-dimensional lattice
Λ\emph{⊥}(\textbf{A}) to a basis \textbf{S}\emph{′}of a
dimension-\emph{m′}superlattice Λ\emph{⊥}(\textbf{A}\emph{′}), and then
immediately sample (one or more times) from a discrete Gaussian over the
superlattice. For the construction and analysis of our schemes, it is
more convenient and modular to treat these operations separately;
however, a naive implementation would be rather inefficient, requiring
at least (\emph{m′})2space and time (where \emph{m′}can be substantially
larger than \emph{m}). Fortunately, the special structure of the
extended basis \textbf{S}\emph{′}, together with the recursive
