\textbf{3.4} \textbf{Randomizing Control}
\end{quote}

Finally, we show how an arborist can randomize its lattice basis, with a
slight loss in quality. This operation is useful for securely delegating
control to another entity, because the resulting basis is still short,
but is (essentially) statistically independent of the original basis.

\begin{quote}
The probabilistic polynomial-time algorithm RandBasis(\textbf{S}\emph{,
s}) takes a basis \textbf{S} of an \emph{m}-dimensional integer lattice
Λ and a parameter \emph{s ≥∥}\textbf{S}\emph{∥· ω}(\emph{√}log
\emph{n}), and outputs a basis \textbf{S}\emph{′}of Λ, generated as
follows.

1. Let \emph{i ←}0. While \emph{i \textless{} m},

(a) Choose \textbf{v} \emph{←}SampleD(\textbf{S}\emph{, s}). If
\textbf{v} is linearly independent of \emph{\{}\textbf{v}1\emph{, . . .
,} \textbf{v}\emph{i\}}, then let \emph{i ←i} + 1 and let
\textbf{v}\emph{i} = \textbf{v}.

2. Output \textbf{S}\emph{′}= ToBasis(\textbf{V}\emph{,}
HNF(\textbf{S})).

In the final step, the (unique) Hermite normal form basis
HNF(\textbf{S}) of Λ is used to ensure that no information particular to
\textbf{S} is leaked by the output; any other publicly available (or
efficiently computable) basis of the lattice could also be used in its
place.
\end{quote}

