For our HIBE schemes, it is convenient and more modular to abstract away
the encryption and decryption processes into a key-encapsulation
mechanism (KEM). The following LWE-based KEM from {[}GPV08{]} (which is
dual to the scheme of Regev {[}Reg05{]}) is well-known. To progress to
our HIBE systems, it is enough to simply understand the KEM interface
(i.e., the forms of the public/secret keys and ciphertexts) and the
conditions on the parameters needed for correct decapsulation.

\begin{quote}
The scheme KEM is parametrized by a modulus \emph{q}, dimension
\emph{m}, and Gaussian parameter \emph{s} that deter-mines the error
distribution \emph{χ} used for encapsulation. As usual, all these
parameters are functions of the LWE dimension \emph{n}, and are
instantiated based on the particular context in which KEM is used.

\emph{•} Gen: Choose \textbf{A} \emph{←}Z\emph{n×m} uniformly at random,
\textbf{x} \emph{←D}Z\emph{m,s} and set \textbf{y} = \textbf{Ax}
\emph{∈}Z\emph{n q}. Output public key \emph{pk} =
\textbf{A}\emph{∥}\textbf{y} \emph{∈}Z\emph{n×}(\emph{m}+1) and secret
key \emph{sk} = \textbf{x}.

\emph{•} Encaps(\emph{pk} = \textbf{A}\emph{∥}\textbf{y}): Choose
\textbf{s} \emph{←}Z\emph{n q}and let

\textbf{b} \emph{←}Noisy\emph{χ}(\textbf{A}\emph{t}\textbf{s}) and
\emph{b′←}Noisy\emph{χ}(\textbf{y}\emph{t}\textbf{s} + \emph{κ ·
⌊q/}2\emph{⌋})\emph{,}\\
where \emph{κ ∈\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}} is a uniformly random bit. Output
\emph{κ} and encapsulation \emph{σ} = \textbf{b}\emph{∥b′∈}Z\emph{m}+1 .

\emph{•} Decaps(\emph{sk} = \textbf{x}\emph{, σ} =
\textbf{b}\emph{∥b′}): Compute \emph{b′−}\textbf{x}\emph{t}\textbf{b}
mod \emph{q} and output 0 if the result is closer to 0 than
to\emph{⌊q/}2\emph{⌋}modulo \emph{q}, and 1 otherwise.

Observe that, conditioned on the public key
\textbf{A}\emph{∥}\textbf{y}, the secret decapsulation key \textbf{x}
has distribution \emph{D}Λ\emph{⊥}\textbf{y}(\textbf{A})\emph{,s}. Later
on, our HIBE schemes will generate a decapsulation key for a given
\textbf{A} and \textbf{y} using \textbf{x}
\emph{←}SampleD(\textbf{S}\emph{,} \textbf{A}\emph{,} \textbf{y}\emph{,
s}), where \textbf{S} is a suitable basis of Λ\emph{⊥}(\textbf{A}). By
Item 5 of Lemma 2.4, the behavior of Decaps is the same (up to
negligible error probability) using such an \textbf{x}.

The following lemma is standard from prior work, e.g., {[}GPV08{]}.
\end{quote}

