\textbf{5.2} \textbf{A Generalized Hierarchical Scheme}
\end{quote}

In this section we describe a generalized hierarchical identity-based
encryption scheme, called GENHIBE, which we later instantiate in
different ways to yield concrete constructions in both the random oracle
and standard models. The generalized system captures the essential
features of all of our HIBE systems, which are the ability to encrypt to
any node in an (implicit) authority tree, and any node's ability to
securely delegate a secret key to any of its descendants.

In GENHIBE there is an identity tree of depth \emph{d} over some
alphabet \emph{ID}, which is identified with the set \emph{ID≤d}in the
natural way: the empty string \emph{ε ∈ID≤d}is the root of the tree, and
an identity \emph{id′}is a descendant of \emph{id} if and only if
\emph{id} is a proper prefix of \emph{id′}. Each identity \emph{id} is
implicitly assigned a public key \emph{pkid} =
\textbf{A}\emph{id∥}\textbf{y}\emph{id} for the KEM scheme described
above in Section 5.1. These public keys must have the property that if
\emph{id′}is a descendant of \emph{id}, then \textbf{A}\emph{id′} is an
extension of \textbf{A}\emph{id} (that is, \textbf{A}\emph{id′} =
\textbf{A}\emph{id∥}¯\textbf{A} for some \textbf{A}). The secret key for
an identity \emph{id} consists of a decapsulation key
\textbf{x}\emph{id} for its KEM public key
\textbf{A}\emph{id∥}\textbf{y}\emph{id}, as well as a relatively short
basis \textbf{S}\emph{id} for the lattice
Λ\emph{⊥}(\textbf{A}\emph{id}). (In our instantiations, the short basis
is used only for delegation, and so it can be omitted for users at the
leaves of the hierarchy.)\\
In GENHIBE we deliberately leave out the Setup algorithm, which must be
provided by an instantiation. The algorithm should output a master
public key \emph{mpk} and the root-level secret key (\textbf{S}\emph{ε,}
\textbf{x}\emph{ε}), as well a description of the mapping from an
identity \emph{id} to a KEM public key \emph{pkid}. These are the only
aspects of GENHIBE that need to be defined when instantiating it. (The
Extract, Encaps, and Decaps algorithms are common to all the
instantiations, and rely on the assignment of KEM public keys to
identities.) Looking ahead, an instantiation of GENHIBE will assign
public keys to identities either via a random oracle, or (in the
standard model) by piecing together different parts of the master public
key.

\begin{quote}
The GENHIBE scheme is parametrized by a few quantities that are indexed
by depth within the hierarchy.

For an identity at depth \emph{t ≥}0 (where \emph{t} = 0 corresponds to
the root),
