Here we give HIBE constructions in the standard model that are secure
under both selective and fully adaptive

attacks. In Section 5.4.1 we describe a binary tree encryption (BTE)
system which is at the heart of all our

various HIBE constructions, and prove its selective security in Section
5.4.2. In Section 5.4.3 and beyond we

\begin{quote}
extend our HIBE to full adaptive security.
\end{quote}

23

\begin{quote}
\textbf{5.4.1} \textbf{Binary Tree Encryption}

Here we instantiate GENHIBE to obtain a standard-model HIBE for a
restricted identity hierarchy in the form of a binary tree, i.e., with
identity alphabet \emph{ID} = \emph{\{}0\emph{,} 1\emph{\}}. This is
essentially the notion of ``binary tree encryption'' (BTE) from
{[}CHK03{]}. Later on we realize selectively and adaptively secure HIBE
schemes for large identity alphabets \emph{ID}, by mapping them onto a
binary tree.
\end{quote}

