(sid-ind-cpa) secure, then so is the resulting HIBE.

\begin{quote}
\textbf{5.4.2} \textbf{Selective Security of} BTE

Recall that in a selective-identity (sid-ind-cpa) attack, the adversary
first has to declare its challenge identity before receiving the master
public key \emph{mpk}. In the security proof, the simulator can
therefore set up \emph{mpk} to depend on the challenge identity. In our
simulation, all matrices in \emph{mpk} will either be undirected, or
controlled by the simulator. As we have seen, a matrix \textbf{A} =
\textbf{A}1\emph{∥. . . ∥}\textbf{A}\emph{ℓ}is undirected if all
\textbf{A}\emph{i} are undirected, while \textbf{A} is controlled if and
only if at least one \textbf{A}\emph{i} is controlled. In the
simulation, \textbf{A}0 will be undirected, so a user secret key for
identity \emph{id} can be constructed if and only if at least one of the
\textbf{A}(\emph{idi}) \emph{i} is controlled. We will set
\end{quote}

24

up these matrices so that: (1) for all \emph{id} that can appear in
Extract queries (i.e., those which differ from \emph{id∗}in

\begin{quote}
at least one position), \textbf{A}\emph{id} is controlled, and (2) for
the challenge identity \emph{id∗}, \textbf{A}\emph{id∗}is undirected.
\end{quote}

\textbf{Theorem 5.3} (Security of BTE)\textbf{.} \emph{There exists a
PPT oracle algorithm (a reduction) S attacking} KEM \emph{(instantiated
with dimension} (\emph{d} + 1)\emph{m and q, α as above) such that, for
any adversary A mounting a sid-ind-cpa attack on} BTE\emph{,}

\textbf{Adv}KEM(\emph{SA}) \emph{≥}\textbf{Adv}\emph{sid-ind-cpa}

\begin{quote}
(\emph{A}) \emph{−}negl(\emph{n})\emph{.}

\emph{Proof.} We construct a reduction \emph{S} attacking KEM, which has
