\end{quote}

on the fly. Observe that if we had better (i.e., tight lower \emph{and}
upper) bounds on \emph{pE} in the first place (e.g.,

\emph{\textbar pE −}∆\emph{d\textbar{} \textless{}} ∆\emph{d/S} always),
we would not need this approximation/abort step at all, since (5.6) and
hence (5.7) followed directly by these better bounds. The good news is
that the analysis of \emph{H} from {[}BB04b{]} provides such better
bounds for Pr{[}\emph{E}{]}, resp. \emph{pE}. The bad news is that this
comes at a price: using the analysis

of {[}BB04b{]}, \emph{\textbar pE −}∆\emph{d\textbar/}∆\emph{d}depends
in an inversely polynomial way on \emph{Q}, the number of user secret
key queries. Hence, to achieve \emph{\textbar pE −}∆\emph{d\textbar{}
\textless{}} ∆\emph{d/S}, we would need to choose fixed \emph{H}
parameters that work for \emph{arbitrary} polynomial values of \emph{Q}.
Of course, in an asymptotic sense, we already do consider arbitrary

\begin{quote}
polynomial values of \emph{Q}, because \emph{A} may make up to \emph{Q}
user secret key queries. However, in a concrete sense, the number of
(online) user secret key queries will typically be much lower than the
inverse of \emph{A}'s distinguishing advantage. Hence, when considering
concrete parameters, we can work with much smaller \emph{H} parameters
when implementing our artificial abort step, at the price of a slower
reduction. This is why we
\end{quote}
